  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions continue along with much warmer air \narriving through the start of the weekend.\n\nAs an upper-level ridge across the Plains builds eastward through \nthe remainder of this week, it will continue to direct sprawling \nsurface high pressure over our region. This surface high however \nwill eventually become more centered to our south and east late this \nweek and especially into the start of the weekend. \n\nDry conditions are expected to continue right into the start of the \nweekend. This will be accompanied by warming temperatures with the \nwarmth looking to peak on Friday as some areas top out near 70 \ndegrees (some 10-15 degrees above average for this time of year). It \nwill still be chilly at night, with lows in the upper 20s to 30s. A \nrather dry air mass looks to remain in place due to the upper-level \nridge and surface high, however the surface dew points should start \nto increase especially on Saturday as some moisture begins to \nincrease as a light onshore flow becomes more established.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring a potential coastal low for the second \nhalf of the weekend into early next week. \n\nAfter surface high pressure shifts offshore on Saturday, most \nguidance depict a coastal low developing off the coast of the \nSoutheast U.S. over the weekend. While there are great differences\namongst the timing, track and intensity of this potential system,\nthere is a general consensus in a low developing. In fact, AI \nguidance has been fairly consistent with the low tracking \nover/within close vicinity of the Mid-Atlantic while other ensemble\nguidance keeps the system well offshore. While it is way too early\nfor specifics of what 'potential' impacts may be felt around \nthe region, it is worth watching as this may be the next chance \nto bring some beneficial rainfall to the region. PoPs are \ncurrently around 30-50%.",
